  "content": {
    "id": "p33-q14-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "14",
    "prompt": "Consider $(1+x)^4(1+x)^4=(1+x)^8$. Show that\n$$\\binom{4}{0}\\binom{4}{2}+\\binom{4}{1}\\binom{4}{1}+\\binom{4}{2}\\binom{4}{0}=\\binom{8}{2}.$$",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\displaystyle \\binom{4}{0}\\binom{4}{2}+\\binom{4}{1}\\binom{4}{1}+\\binom{4}{2}\\binom{4}{0}=\\binom{8}{2}$.\n\nCompare coefficients of $x^2$.",
      "worked": "In the product $(1+x)^4(1+x)^4$, the $x^2$ terms come from $1\\times x^2$, $x\\times x$ and $x^2\\times1$. Their coefficient is\n$$\\binom{4}{0}\\binom{4}{2}+\\binom{4}{1}\\binom{4}{1}+\\binom{4}{2}\\binom{4}{0}.$$\nIn $(1+x)^8$, the coefficient of $x^2$ is $\\binom{8}{2}$. Since the two expressions are identical, equating these coefficients gives\n$$\\begin{align*}\\binom{4}{0}\\binom{4}{2}+\\binom{4}{1}\\binom{4}{1}+\\binom{4}{2}\\binom{4}{0}&=\\binom{8}{2}.\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
